{"v":1,"accession":"PRJEB81546","title":"Microbial ecology in long-term up-flow anaerobic reactor operation treating municipal wastewater at low temperatures and variable loadings","description":"Microbial community characterization of the low temperature in anaerobic granules were presented in this study. Granules were taken from up-flow granulated anaerobic reactors at 12, 8.5, 5.5 and 2.5 C which were operated continuously over 1025 days by applying a stepwise increase of organic loading rate by intermittent increases to 15.2±0.2 g COD·l-1·d-1. In this study, methanogen communities showed temperature change effected the degradation pathway of organic matter with acetoclastic methanogen and H2-dependent methylotrophic methanogens played significant roles. At 2.5 °C, acetate might be the main precursor of methanogenesis. This successful operation at low temperatures along with microbial community confirmation, the prevalence of psychrotolerant communities was observed throughout long-term reactor operation, indicating the long-term adaptation of the microbial community in granulated biomass through changes in the community structure and low temperature anaerobic treatment potential in pilot and/or full-scale application for treating municipal wastewater.","center":"Department of Chemistry, Bioscience and Environmental Engineering","data_type":"Other","submission_date":"2024/10/29 00:00","sample_cols":["accession","title","tier","context","collection_date","collection_year","country","region","place_id","place","location_status","lat","lon","submitted_location","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","eligibility_reason"],"samples":[["SAMEA116305380","AArcT12O8","confirmed_urban","municipal_wastewater",null,2020,"NO",null,null,null,"unresolved",null,null,"Norway",null,null,null,null,"explicit urban setting at sample level"],["SAMEA116305381","AArcT12O15","confirmed_urban","municipal_wastewater",null,2020,"NO",null,null,null,"unresolved",